description:
pkix-path/src/lib.rs:748–776

The function is:

    fn prune_policy_tree(tree: &mut Vec<PolicyNode>, cert_depth: usize) {
        let mut d = cert_depth;
        while d > 0 {
            d -= 1;
            if d == 0 { break; }  // depth-0 root is always kept
            ...
        }
    }

The while loop condition is 'd > 0', so after 'd -= 1' the only way to reach the 'if d == 0 { break }' is when d was 1 before the decrement.  This is equivalent to 'while d > 1' as the outer condition:

    while d > 1 {
        d -= 1;
        ...
    }

The extra 'break' inside the loop at d==0 is never reached — the while guard 'd > 0' after 'd -= 1' would exit on the next iteration anyway.  A future reader must trace through both the while condition AND the inner break to understand what the loop does.  The loop structure obscures the invariant that d is always >= 1 when the body executes.  Simplify to 'while d > 1 { d -= 1; ... }' and remove the dead break.
